========================================================================================================
S2. ITEM 5 -- is tau_mem = v/a0 FORCED? Enumerate every dimensionally-allowed monomial
========================================================================================================
  tau = c^p v^q a0^s r^t Omega^m,  require Omega tau = a/a0 on circles identically:
    solution: [{p: 0, q: 1 - m, s: -1, t: m}]
  [OK  ] the identity forces p = 0 (c EXCLUDED), s = -1, q = 1-m, t = m -- a ONE-PARAMETER family tau_m = (v/a0)(r Omega/v)^m, with m free
  [OK  ] and [L]^0 [T]^1 -- the length/time dimensions come out right AUTOMATICALLY for every m, so dimensional analysis alone constrains NOTHING here; the circular identity is what does the work, and it still leaves a free function

  spot-check members of the family. tau_m is written with Omega UNSUBSTITUTED so the
  (r Omega/v)^m factor stays visible; the circular relation Omega = v/r is imposed only to
  evaluate the identity, where that factor becomes exactly 1:
    m = -2: tau_m = v**3/(Omega**2*a_0*r**2)   ->   Omega tau_m |_circ = v**2/(a_0*r)  = a/a0
  [OK  ] m = -2: a genuinely DIFFERENT memory time reproduces a/a0 on circles exactly -- the members are inequivalent functionals that agree on the only constrained slice
    m = -1: tau_m = v**2/(Omega*a_0*r)   ->   Omega tau_m |_circ = v**2/(a_0*r)  = a/a0
  [OK  ] m = -1: a genuinely DIFFERENT memory time reproduces a/a0 on circles exactly -- the members are inequivalent functionals that agree on the only constrained slice
    m = +0: tau_m = v/a_0   ->   Omega tau_m |_circ = v**2/(a_0*r)  = a/a0
  [OK  ] m = +0: a genuinely DIFFERENT memory time reproduces a/a0 on circles exactly -- the members are inequivalent functionals that agree on the only constrained slice
    m = +1: tau_m = Omega*r/a_0   ->   Omega tau_m |_circ = v**2/(a_0*r)  = a/a0
  [OK  ] m = +1: a genuinely DIFFERENT memory time reproduces a/a0 on circles exactly -- the members are inequivalent functionals that agree on the only constrained slice
    m = +2: tau_m = Omega**2*r**2/(a_0*v)   ->   Omega tau_m |_circ = v**2/(a_0*r)  = a/a0
  [OK  ] m = +2: a genuinely DIFFERENT memory time reproduces a/a0 on circles exactly -- the members are inequivalent functionals that agree on the only constrained slice

  THE SPREAD OFF CIRCLES, on exact closure-solving orbits. There r Omega/v = v_t/v, so
  the family separates as (v_t/v)^m. Omega_E = v_t/r is used for every member, so this
  isolates the tau-freedom alone. Evaluated at the orbital phase where v_t/v is smallest:
        e  min v_t/v    arg m=-2    arg m=-1     arg m=0     arg m=1     arg m=2  max/min  mu spread
     0.00    1.00000     0.42398     0.42398     0.42398     0.42398     0.42398     1.00      1.000
     0.10    0.99042     0.42778     0.42368     0.41962     0.41560     0.41162     1.04      1.033
     0.20    0.96175     0.43620     0.41952     0.40347     0.38804     0.37319     1.17      1.144
     0.40    0.84819     0.48740     0.41341     0.35065     0.29742     0.25227     1.93      1.791
     0.60    0.66304     0.60101     0.39849     0.26422     0.17519     0.11615     5.17      4.465
  [OK  ] the one-parameter tau-family is EXACTLY degenerate on circles (argument spread 1.000000000) and separates by 5x in the kernel argument and 4.46x in mu itself by e = 0.6. 'The circular slice cannot see the freedom' is therefore not a rhetorical point: it is a factor 4.46 in the predicted g_bar at fixed acceleration, from a choice nothing in the route fixes

  CONCLUSION OF ITEM 5, stated flatly. tau_mem = v/a0 is NOT forced. What IS forced, and
  this is a real result, is that c CANNOT APPEAR: any tau containing c fails the circular
  identity identically in v. That is the formal version of 'the frame must be locally
  dragged'. But the surviving family is (v/a0) x [any dimensionless function equal to 1 on
  circles], which is infinite-dimensional -- monomials alone already give a one-parameter
  family. Off circles the route therefore PINS NOTHING. This is the same corner-blindness
  the corpus already records for omega_c (MI_OFFCIRCULAR_COMPLETION_2026.md, C1): the only
  constrained slice is the circular one, and it cannot see the freedom.

