S3. ITEM 2 -- tau_mem = v/a0 for real systems vs the corpus's [1 Myr .. 17.5 Gyr] window
========================================================================================================
  window provenance: MI_OFFCIRCULAR_COMPLETION_2026.md verdict table -- omega_c FREE but
  bounded in [1/kappa = 17.5 Gyr (raw dS correlator) .. 1 Myr (d1 above-band pole)].
  NOTE ON PROVENANCE, because it changes how hard a 'falsification' here bites: those two
  endpoints are the two BATH-NATIVE candidate scales, and the corpus's own language at
  both ends is 'door ~dies' -- i.e. the OBSERVABLE (dwarf sigma-hysteresis) magnitude goes
  to zero there, not that the theory becomes inconsistent. So landing outside is a real
  problem for the DOOR, and only a weak problem for CONSISTENCY. Both are reported.

  case                                  v_rel      tau [canonical]  in window?     tau [alternate]  in window?
  Milky-Way-like disk star             220.0k           74.482 Myr         YES           61.695 Myr         YES
  dwarf-spheroidal star                 10.0k            3.386 Myr         YES            2.804 Myr         YES
  galaxy-cluster member               1000.0k          338.555 Myr         YES          280.432 Myr         YES
  wide binary internal, 10 kAU           0.5k            0.169 Myr          NO            0.140 Myr          NO
  Earth (heliocentric orbital)          29.8k           10.089 Myr         YES            8.357 Myr         YES

  span of tau_mem demanded across these five cases: 3.30 dex (v_rel from 0.5 to 1000 km/s)
  corpus window width: 4.24 dex

  input-independence of the wide-binary row: the estimate above used 0.5 km/s, but the
  CLOSURE's own self-consistent circular speed at 10 kAU around 1 Msun is 0.361 km/s,
  giving tau_mem = 0.122 Myr canonical / 0.101 Myr alternate -- further BELOW the floor,
  so the conclusion does not depend on the 0.5 km/s estimate.
  [OK  ] self-consistent wide-binary tau_mem = 0.122 Myr is 8.2x below the 1 Myr floor, vs 5.9x for the 0.5 km/s estimate -- the finding is robust to the input speed and is conservative as stated
  [OK  ] THE WIDE BINARY FALLS OUTSIDE, below the floor, on BOTH footings: 0.169 Myr canonical (5.9x below the 1 Myr floor) and 0.140 Myr alternate (7.1x below). Under the LITERAL reading of the corpus bound this FALSIFIES the prescription; under the provenance-corrected reading it says the wide-binary memory door is dead, not the theory
  [OK  ] the dwarf-spheroidal case clears the floor by only 3.4x (3.39 Myr) -- it is marginal, not comfortable
  [OK  ] the other four cases (MW star, dSph star, cluster member, Earth) all land INSIDE the window on both footings, spanning 2.8 Myr - 339 Myr, comfortably inside the door-relevant 0.4 Gyr scale the corpus singles out
  [OK  ] BUT NOTE THE CATEGORY CHANGE, and it is the honest headline of item 2: the corpus's omega_c is a UNIVERSAL CONSTANT awaiting a pin; Route B makes it a STATE FUNCTION spanning 3.30 dex. The route therefore does not 'pin omega_c' -- it ELIMINATES omega_c as a constant and replaces it with a rule. That is only a reduction in free content if the rule is itself forced, which S2 shows it is not off circles

========================================================================================================
S4. THE FRAME'S DRAG LEVEL -- three locks. (a) is re-verified; (b) and (c) are new.
========================================================================================================
  (a) COSMIC (CMB) FRAME -- re-verified from the framework's own RAR.
      deep regime g_obs = sqrt(g_bar a0)  =>  d log10 g_obs = 0.5 d log10 a0_eff
      framework's own RAR scatter 0.108 dex  =>  TOTAL a0 budget 0.216 dex (factor 1.64), with nothing left over for distance, inclination or M/L
  [OK  ] d log g_obs / d log a0 = 1/2 in the deep regime -- so the 2x in the budget is derived, not assumed (and it is the same for alpha=1 and alpha=2, both of which give g_obs -> sqrt(g_bar a0))
  [OK  ] CMB-frame speed EXCLUDED: peculiar velocities 100-1000 km/s = 1.00 dex of injected a0 variation vs a 0.216 dex budget = 4.6x over

  (b) LOCAL-MATTER DRAG (frame co-moving with the surrounding matter) -- NEW, and it is a
      squeeze from the OTHER side. If the passive u is dragged by the LOCAL matter, then a
      disk star's speed relative to u is not its circular speed but its RANDOM velocity,
      because the local matter co-rotates. Then a0_eff = a0 (v_circ/v_rel) is too LARGE by
      v_circ/sigma, and it varies with stellar population.
      population                     v_circ    sigma  v_circ/sigma  dex in a0_eff  x budget
      thin-disk young                  220k      20k         11.00          1.041      4.82
      thin-disk old                    220k      35k          6.29          0.798      3.70
      thick disk                       200k      55k          3.64          0.561      2.60
      dSph (pressure-supported)         12k      10k          1.20          0.079      0.37
  [OK  ] local-matter drag mis-scales a0 for every disk population by 3.6-11.0x (0.56-1.04 dex), i.e. 2.6-4.8x the whole a0 budget -- a frame dragged by the local matter is EXCLUDED
  [OK  ] and it is not even a constant offset that could be absorbed into kappa: the population spread alone is 0.48 dex = 2.2x the budget, so it would show up as RAR scatter within a single galaxy
      => the frame must be at rest w.r.t. the ORBIT'S CENTRE (the bound system's
         barycentre), not w.r.t. the local matter and not w.r.t. the cosmos. Route B is
         squeezed to exactly one prescription, and it is a NONLOCAL, MEMBERSHIP-DEPENDENT
         one: 'the barycentre of whichever bound system this particle orbits'.

  (c) HIERARCHY -- the sharpest lock, and it is a NO-GO for a LOCAL frame field u(x).
      A local u assigns ONE frame per spacetime point. Take two stars at the SAME point:
      one bound to a satellite dwarf, one to the host disk. The barycentre prescription
      needs u at rest w.r.t. the DWARF for the first and w.r.t. the HOST for the second.
      A single-valued local field cannot do both. Quantify the cost of choosing the host:
      satellite v_orb (dwarf-internal)    host-frame v   factor  a0_eff dex  sigma_pred/sigma_obs
                                  12 km/s          100k      8.3      -0.921                 0.589
                                  12 km/s          150k     12.5      -1.097                 0.532
                                  12 km/s          200k     16.7      -1.222                 0.495
                                  12 km/s          300k     25.0      -1.398                 0.447
  [OK  ] deep-regime isothermal relation sigma^4 = (4/81) G M a0 (Milgrom) gives d log sigma / d log a0 = 1/4, verified symbolically -- so a factor F error in a0_eff moves a dwarf's predicted dispersion by F^(1/4)
  [OK  ] choosing the HOST barycentre makes satellite dwarfs 8-25x too Newtonian in a0_eff, so their predicted dispersions drop from 10.0 to 5.9-4.5 km/s -- 4.6-6.1sigma against a ~0.9 km/s measurement error. Satellite dwarfs DO sit on the relation, so a host-dragged local frame is excluded by data that already exists
  [OK  ] and the contradiction is unavoidable for a single-valued local u: the two co-located stars need frames differing by 25x in speed. Escaping it requires a frame that is PARTICLE-dependent (i.e. not a field at all), which is not a field theory, or a genuinely nonlocal membership rule, which the framework's passive u does not have

