       1.0e-04                4.0682e+01         0.4240  9.595e+01
  [OK  ] candidates A and E are FATAL on radial motion: as v_t/v falls 1 -> 1e-4 the required acceleration grows by 96x and diverges as (v_t/v)^(-1/2) in the deep regime -- a radial faller would need unbounded acceleration to feel the same g_bar. This is a genuine singularity of the prescription, not a small-parameter artefact

    candidate G = |adot|/|a| CURES it: for radial infall adot is parallel to a and
    |adot|/|a| = |A'| v_r/A != 0. But it gets the WRONG argument, and by a factor that
    grows without bound. Deep regime A = sqrt(GM a0)/r so |A'|/A = 1/r, and energy
    conservation from rest at r0 gives v_r^2 = 2 A r ln(r0/r), hence
        Omega_G tau_mem = v_r^2/(r a0) = 2 (A/a0) ln(r0/r) = 2 u ln(r0/r).
  [OK  ] symbolically: Omega_G tau_mem = log((r_0/r)**(2*sqrt(GM)/(sqrt(a_0)*r))) = 2 u ln(r0/r) exactly on deep-regime radial infall -- so G is off by the factor 2 ln(r0/r), which is 1 only at r0/r = e^(1/2) = 1.65 and grows logarithmically without bound as the faller starts further out
          r0/r   2 ln(r0/r)  <- the factor by which G mis-states the argument
          1.65        1.002
          3.00        2.197
         10.00        4.605
        100.00        9.210
       1000.00       13.816
  [OK  ] the factor runs 1.00 -> 13.8 over r0/r = 1.65 -> 1e3, i.e. G is exact at one radius ratio and wrong by an order of magnitude for a faller from 1000x out. Not fatal, but not the law either -- and it costs a THIRD time derivative (jerk) in the action

  ITEM 3 VERDICT, both ways. Of the four candidates: B is a tautology (v cancels, the
  frame does nothing, and the resulting law is a LOCAL function of |xddot| -- which is
  exactly what corrected Theorem 3 excludes from having any local action). A and E are
  FATAL on radial motion (divergent required acceleration). G is the only non-tautological
  candidate that survives radial motion, and it is wrong there by 2 ln(r0/r), unbounded,
  while also failing off circles for bound orbits. NONE survives.

========================================================================================================
S6. ITEM 4 -- ghost-freedom / positivity, and the inequality Route B must close
========================================================================================================
  (i) WHAT THE HERGLOTZ-NEVANLINNA ARGUMENT ACTUALLY BUYS, and where it stops.
      The corpus's positivity result is about a FIXED kernel K(z) = 1 - INT rho(s)ds/(z+s)
      with rho >= 0: that makes K a Herglotz-Nevanlinna function, gives ||K|| <= 1 on the
      physical domain z > 0, and makes the LINEAR response causal-retarded and passive.
      Every one of those statements is a statement about a linear, state-INDEPENDENT map.
      Route B makes the corner omega_c = a0/v_rel depend on the particle's own speed, so
      the response is a NONLINEAR functional of the trajectory. Concretely: a one-parameter
      family {K_{omega_c}} can be Herglotz member-by-member while the composite map
      x(t) -> F[x](t) is neither linear nor passive, because passivity is a statement about
      the map, not about the members. NOTHING in the corpus's positivity chain survives
      the substitution automatically. That is a precise statement of what is NOT
      established; it is not a claim that positivity fails.

  (ii) THE CONCRETE TEST: the function-vs-average inequality, which is the gap Theorem 8
       actually names -- K(<Box_u>_u) != <K(Box_u)>_u -- and it is UNTOUCHED by rescaling
       the argument. Route B changes WHAT the argument is; the inequality is about
       commuting K past an average. Sign it with concavity.
       K_2(z) = sqrt(z/(1+z)) ;  K_2''(z) = (-z - 1/4)/(z**(3/2)*sqrt(z + 1)*(z**2 + 2*z + 1))
       K_2''(z) * 4 z^(3/2) (1+z)^(5/2) = -4*z - 1   (the prefactor is > 0 on z>0)
  [OK  ] K_2'' = -(4z+1)/[4 z^(3/2)(1+z)^(5/2)] < 0 for all z > 0: the alpha=2 kernel is STRICTLY CONCAVE on its physical domain, so by Jensen K_2(<z>) >= <K_2(z)> with equality iff z is deterministic -- i.e. iff the trajectory is SINGLE-FREQUENCY. This is Milgrom 2022 PRD 106:064060's single-frequency restriction, re-derived as a convexity statement
  [OK  ] numerically confirmed over 16 decades: max K_2'' = -1.000e-24 < 0
  [OK  ] the retired alpha=1 kernel is concave too (max K_1'' = -3.750e-21), so the Jensen direction is KERNEL-INDEPENDENT across both kernels the corpus has used

       Now the SIZE of the gap on real orbits: z = (|a|/a0)^2, averaged over the orbit
       with residence-time weights from the exact turning-point states.

